    {
      "id": "01",
      "title": "std::move is a cast",
      "question": "What does std::move actually do at runtime?",
      "scenes": [
        {
          "type": "question",
          "say": "Move semantics, question one. What does standard move actually do at runtime? Pause and answer out loud.",
          "pauseAfter": 3,
          "props": {
            "question": "What does <em>std::move</em> actually do at runtime?"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "say": "Here is the whole implementation. It takes any reference, strips the reference from the type, and returns a static cast to an rvalue reference. That is all. Nothing is copied and nothing is moved. The transfer happens later, when that rvalue selects a move constructor.",
          "props": {
            "title": "The whole of <em>std::move</em>",
            "file": "<utility>",
            "tag": "compile-time only",
            "size": 24,
            "width": 1180,
            "code": "template <typename T>\nconstexpr std::remove_reference_t<T>&& move(T&& t) noexcept {\n    return static_cast<std::remove_reference_t<T>&&>(t);\n}",
            "highlights": [
              {
                "at": "takes",
                "lines": "2",
                "note": "Accepts anything: a forwarding reference"
              },
              {
                "at": "strips",
                "lines": "2",
                "note": "remove_reference_t drops & or &&"
              },
              {
                "at": "returns",
                "lines": "3",
                "note": "Just a static_cast to T&&"
              },
              {
                "at": "nothing",
                "lines": "1-4",
                "note": "No data is copied or moved here"
              },
              {
                "at": "later",
                "lines": "3",
                "note": "The move constructor does the transfer"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "graph",
          "say": "Every expression has a value category. Some have identity: a named variable you can take the address of. Others are pure temporaries, like a literal, or a function returning by value. The third kind sits in between: it has identity, but it is expiring, so its resources can be stolen. Standard move turns the first kind into the expiring kind. And because that is an rvalue, the move constructor can bind to it.",
          "props": {
            "title": "Value categories, <em>and where move lands</em>",
