    {
      "id": "02",
      "title": "Three silent mistakes",
      "question": "Name three ways std::move quietly goes wrong.",
      "scenes": [
        {
          "type": "question",
          "say": "Question two. Name three ways standard move quietly goes wrong. Pause and answer out loud.",
          "pauseAfter": 3,
          "props": {
            "question": "Name three ways <em>std::move</em> quietly goes wrong."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "compare",
          "say": "Mistake one: moving a return value. Returning a local by name lets the compiler build it directly in the caller's memory, at zero cost. Wrapping it in standard move forces a real move, and blocks that optimization.",
          "props": {
            "title": "Mistake one: <em>moving a return</em>",
            "left": {
              "at": "wrapping",
              "label": "Don't",
              "title": "Blocks NRVO",
              "tone": "bad",
              "code": "std::string make() {\n    std::string result = \"hello\";\n    return std::move(result);  // forces a move\n}"
            },
            "right": {
              "at": "returning",
              "label": "Do",
              "title": "NRVO or implicit move",
              "tone": "good",
              "code": "std::string make() {\n    std::string result = \"hello\";\n    return result;  // built in the caller\n}"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "compare",
          "say": "Mistake two: moving a const object. Standard move then yields a const rvalue, which cannot bind to the move constructor. So the copy constructor quietly wins. It compiles, and it copies. Drop the const if you mean to move.",
          "props": {
            "title": "Mistake two: <em>moving a const</em>",
            "left": {
              "at": "const",
              "label": "Silent copy",
              "title": "const T&& → copy",
              "tone": "bad",
              "code": "const std::string s = \"data\";\nstd::string t = std::move(s);  // COPY ctor",
              "body": "Benchmark: ~7.5 ms, same as a deep copy."
            },
            "right": {
              "at": "drop",
              "label": "Real move",
              "title": "Non-const source",
              "tone": "good",
              "code": "std::string s = \"data\";\nstd::string t = std::move(s);  // move ctor",
              "body": "Benchmark: ~1.1 ms, about 7× faster."
            },
            "verdict": {
              "at": "copies",
              "text": "const + std::move = a copy.",
              "code": "no warning"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "points",
          "say": "Mistake three: using a moved-from object. It is valid but unspecified, so destroy it or assign to it, nothing else. And a bonus rule: mark move operations no-except, or a vector will copy instead of move when it grows. Inside a move constructor, standard exchange steals a member and resets it in one step.",
          "props": {
            "title": "Mistake three, <em>plus two rules</em>",
            "items": [
              {
                "at": "unspecified",
                "label": "Moved-from",
                "title": "Valid but unspecified",
                "body": "Only destroy or reassign it.",
                "code": "v = {};  // ok"
              },
              {
                "at": "bonus",
                "label": "noexcept",
                "title": "Or vector copies",
                "body": "Reallocation needs a no-throw move.",
                "code": "T(T&&) noexcept"
              },
              {
                "at": "steals",
                "label": "Idiom",
                "title": "std::exchange",
                "body": "Steal and reset in one step.",
                "code": "p_(std::exchange(o.p_, nullptr))"
              }
            ]
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "03",
      "title": "std::move vs std::forward",
      "question": "When do you use std::forward instead of std::move?",
      "scenes": [
        {
          "type": "question",
          "say": "Question three. When do you use standard forward instead of standard move? Pause and answer out loud.",
          "pauseAfter": 3,
          "props": {
            "question": "When do you use <em>std::forward</em> instead of <em>std::move</em>?"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "compare",
          "say": "Standard move is an unconditional cast. Use it when you are done with an object. Standard forward is conditional. Inside a template that takes a forwarding reference, it casts back to an rvalue only if the caller passed an rvalue, so the right overload is chosen.",
          "props": {
            "title": "Always rvalue, or <em>as received</em>",
            "left": {
              "at": "unconditional",
              "label": "std::move",
              "title": "Unconditional cast",
              "code": "void sink(Widget w);\nsink(std::move(widget));  // done with it",
              "body": "Normal code: transfer ownership."
            },
            "right": {
              "at": "conditional",
              "label": "std::forward<T>",
              "title": "Preserves the category",
              "code": "template <typename T>\nvoid relay(T&& arg) {\n    target(std::forward<T>(arg));\n}",
              "body": "Templates with forwarding references only."
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "points",
          "say": "This works through reference collapsing. When an lvalue is passed, T is deduced as an lvalue reference, and T double-ampersand collapses to an lvalue reference. If either side is an lvalue reference, the result is one. Only rvalue reference on rvalue reference stays an rvalue reference.",
          "props": {
            "title": "Reference <em>collapsing</em>",
            "items": [
              {
                "at": "collapsing",
                "label": "& + &",
                "title": "T& &  → T&",
                "body": "Lvalue wins.",
                "code": "lvalue"
              },
              {
                "at": "deduced",
                "label": "& + &&",
                "title": "T& && → T&",
                "body": "Lvalue passed in: T = Widget&.",
                "code": "lvalue"
              },
              {
                "at": "either",
                "label": "&& + &",
                "title": "T&& & → T&",
                "body": "Any & makes it &.",
                "code": "lvalue"
              },
              {
                "at": "only",
                "label": "&& + &&",
                "title": "T&& && → T&&",
                "body": "The only rvalue result.",
                "code": "rvalue"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "recap",
          "say": "To recap. Standard move only casts. Don't move return values, or const objects. Mark moves no-except. Forward exactly once per argument, and only in templates. And treat moved-from objects as dead.",
          "props": {
            "title": "Move semantics, <em>in five lines</em>",
            "items": [
              {
                "at": "casts",
                "text": "std::move only casts to an rvalue.",
                "code": "static_cast<T&&>"
              },
              {
                "at": "return",
                "text": "Don't move return values or const objects.",
                "code": "NRVO · silent copy"
              },
              {
                "at": "mark",
                "text": "Mark move operations noexcept.",
                "code": "vector growth"
              },
              {
                "at": "once",
                "text": "std::forward once per argument, in templates.",
                "code": "T&& + deduced T"
              },
              {
                "at": "dead",
                "text": "Treat moved-from objects as dead.",
                "code": "destroy / reassign"
              }
            ]
          }
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
